"00_external_memory.mst — the FFI marshalling runtime (DolphinDart DD6c).

 HAND-WRITTEN, unlike the rest of st/prims. Loaded before the generated
 libraries; they call into it.

 The model, settled by the DD6 RECT gate: external memory is a block outside the
 Dart heap, addressed by integer. Nothing here is ever moved by a GC because
 nothing here is IN the collected heap — the property Dolphin's own FFI silently
 assumes and would not get on a moving-collector VM.

 The allocator is Win32's own, reached through the same FFI floor as everything
 else. No new primitive was needed for any of this."

"The runtime's OWN prims. Deliberately not taken from the generated libraries:
 Dolphin declares LocalFree but never LocalAlloc (its image allocates through
 its own heap), and infrastructure this layer depends on should not be hostage
 to what the corpus happened to declare."
Object subclass: Win32Rt [
    Win32Rt class >> localAlloc: flags size: n [
        <primitive: FFI function: #LocalAlloc ret: #g args: #( g g )>
    ]
    Win32Rt class >> localFree: p [
        <primitive: FFI function: #LocalFree ret: #g args: #( g )>
    ]
    Win32Rt class >> lstrlenW: p [
        <primitive: FFI function: #lstrlenW ret: #g args: #( g )>
    ]
]

Object subclass: ExternalMemory [
    "`owned` is the lifetime bit, not a subclass: a VIEW onto a nested field
     must be an instance of that field's own struct class (PAINTSTRUCT's
     rcPaint is a RECT, with RECT's accessors), so view-ness cannot live in the
     class. Freeing a view would free the enclosing block."
    | address size owned |

    "LMEM_FIXED | LMEM_ZEROINIT = 0 | 64."
    ExternalMemory class >> new: nBytes [
        | p |
        nBytes <= 0 ifTrue: [ ^self error: 'ExternalMemory new: needs a positive size' ].
        p := Win32Rt localAlloc: 64 size: nBytes.
        p = 0 ifTrue: [ ^self error: 'ExternalMemory: LocalAlloc failed, GetLastError=',
                                     Win32 lastError printString ].
        ^self basicNew setAddress: p size: nBytes owned: true
    ]

    "Dolphin's own struct-allocation selector: 'answer a new instance
     containing its own buffer' (`ExternalStructure class >> newBuffer`,
     `^self newFixed: self byteSize`). Its translated code says `RECTL
     newBuffer`, never `RECTL new: 16` — `View>>getRect`, `Rectangle`,
     `Menu`, PAINTSTRUCT and WINDOWPLACEMENT all allocate through it.

     Defined HERE, on the shared superclass, rather than emitted into each of
     the generated struct files: Dolphin defines it once on the struct
     metaclass and inherits it, and every generated struct already overrides
     `new` with its own `sizeInBytes`, so one inherited method answers
     correctly for all of them. Adding it per-file would be the same method
     copied N times, and a struct generated later would silently lack it."
    ExternalMemory class >> newBuffer [
        | buf |
        buf := self new.
        "SET THE SIZE FIELD, if the struct has one.

         Many Win32 structs carry their own byte size in a leading field so
         the API can tell which version of the struct it was handed, and the
         call FAILS when it is zero. `SetMenuInfo` with a zeroed `cbSize`
         answers false and changes nothing — it does not raise, and
         `Menu>>applyFlags` then beeps, which is a long way from the cause.

         Dolphin does this too: its sized structs inherit a base whose
         `defineFields` puts the size first and whose creation fills it in.
         Doing it HERE, once, covers the family — eight structs in the
         generated floor have `cbSize` and WINDOWPLACEMENT has `length` —
         rather than waiting for each to be found by a call that quietly did
         nothing.

         Guarded by `respondsTo:` rather than a list of class names, so a
         struct generated later is covered the day it appears."
        (buf respondsTo: #cbSize:) ifTrue: [ buf cbSize: self sizeInBytes ].
        (buf respondsTo: #length:) ifTrue: [ buf length: self sizeInBytes ].
        ^buf
    ]

    "A typed window onto memory someone else owns."
    ExternalMemory class >> viewOn: parent at: byteOffset size: n [
        ^self basicNew setAddress: parent address + byteOffset size: n owned: false
    ]

    "A NON-OWNING window onto a RAW ADDRESS — Dolphin's `ExternalAddress`.

     Windows hands the image bare pointers all the time: lParam on
     WM_WINDOWPOSCHANGED is a WINDOWPOS*, on WM_NOTIFY an NMHDR*. Dolphin
     wraps those in an ExternalAddress, which carries the peek/poke protocol;
     an integer does not, and `PositionEvent` reads through it with
     `uint32AtOffset:`.

     The size is UNKNOWN — the pointer came from Windows with no length — so
     it is recorded as 0 and the accessors do not bounds-check against it.
     That is the honest state of affairs for a raw pointer, and it is why this
     is `fromAddress:` rather than something that pretends to know an extent."
    ExternalMemory class >> fromAddress: anAddressOrMemory [
        "Takes EITHER a raw integer address or something that already has one.
         Dolphin's own code chains them — `WINDOWPOS fromAddress: lParam
         asExternalAddress` hands a wrapped address straight to a struct
         class's `fromAddress:` — and storing an ExternalMemory where an
         integer address belongs fails later, at `address + i - 1`, with a
         doesNotUnderstand on `+` rather than at the call that did it."
        | addr |
        addr := (anAddressOrMemory isKindOf: ExternalMemory)
            ifTrue: [ anAddressOrMemory address ]
            ifFalse: [ anAddressOrMemory ].
        "SIZE 0 — the pointer came from Windows with no length, and that is
         the honest record of it. The accessors do not bounds-check against
         `size`, so nothing is lost.

         An earlier version answered `self sizeInBytes` here and added an
         `ExternalMemory class >> sizeInBytes [ ^0 ]` default for the base.
         That DEFAULT SILENTLY BROKE `newBuffer`: a `self` send from an
         inherited CLASS-side method binds to the defining class's own
         class side, so `newBuffer`'s `self sizeInBytes` started answering 0
         for every struct, `cbSize` was written as 0, and InsertMenuItem
         failed with ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER — a Win32 error a long way from
         a Smalltalk method added for an unrelated reason."
        ^self basicNew setAddress: addr size: 0 owned: false
    ]

    setAddress: p size: n owned: aBool [ address := p. size := n. owned := aBool ]
    isView [ ^owned ~~ true ]
    address [ ^address ]
    size [ ^size ]
    isNull [ ^address isNil or: [ address = 0 ] ]

    "Explicit lifetime. v1 has no finalization (DIALECT_GAPS A7: weak refs and
     mourning are backlog), so whoever allocates frees — and the generated
     wrappers do, for the temporaries they create."
    free [
        (self isNull or: [ self isView ]) ifFalse: [
            Win32Rt localFree: address. address := 0 ]
    ]

    "── Dolphin's OFFSET protocol — ZERO-based ──────────────────────────
     `uint32At:` and its kin here are ONE-based, matching Smalltalk indexing
     and the offsets genstructs emits. Dolphin's struct code also uses an
     `...AtOffset:` family that is ZERO-based, straight from the C offsets:
     `bytes uint32AtOffset: _OffsetOf_wID put: id`.
     Both spellings exist in the corpus, so both exist here, and the `+ 1` is
     the whole of the difference. Getting it backwards reads the neighbouring
     field, which is a wrong ANSWER rather than an error."
    byteAtOffset: i [ ^self byteAt: i + 1 ]
    byteAtOffset: i put: v [ ^self byteAt: i + 1 put: v ]
    uint32AtOffset: i [ ^self uint32At: i + 1 ]
    uint32AtOffset: i put: v [ ^self uint32At: i + 1 put: v ]
    int32AtOffset: i [ ^self int32At: i + 1 ]
    uint16AtOffset: i [ ^self uint16At: i + 1 ]
    uint16AtOffset: i put: v [ ^self uint16At: i + 1 put: v ]
    int16AtOffset: i [ ^self int16At: i + 1 ]
    intptrAtOffset: i [ ^self intptrAt: i + 1 ]
    intptrAtOffset: i put: v [ ^self intptrAt: i + 1 put: v ]
    uintPtrAtOffset: i [ ^self intptrAt: i + 1 ]
    uintPtrAtOffset: i put: v [ ^self intptrAt: i + 1 put: v ]

    byteAt: i [ ^Win32 peek: address + i - 1 ]
    byteAt: i put: v [ ^Win32 poke: address + i - 1 put: v ]

    "Little-endian composites. Peek is byte-wise — all an Alien-shaped floor
     guarantees — so the widths are composed here rather than assumed."
    uint32At: i [
        | v b |
        v := 0.
        3 to: 0 by: -1 do: [ :k | b := self byteAt: i + k. v := (v bitShift: 8) bitOr: b ].
        ^v
    ]
    int32At: i [
        | v |
        v := self uint32At: i.
        ^v > 2147483647 ifTrue: [ v - 4294967296 ] ifFalse: [ v ]
    ]
    uint32At: i put: v [
        | x |
        x := v.
        0 to: 3 do: [ :k | self byteAt: i + k put: (x bitAnd: 255). x := x bitShift: -8 ].
        ^v
    ]
    int8At: i [
        | v |
        v := self byteAt: i.
        ^v > 127 ifTrue: [ v - 256 ] ifFalse: [ v ]
    ]
    uint16At: i [
        ^(self byteAt: i) bitOr: ((self byteAt: i + 1) bitShift: 8)
    ]
    int16At: i [
        | v |
        v := self uint16At: i.
        ^v > 32767 ifTrue: [ v - 65536 ] ifFalse: [ v ]
    ]
    uint16At: i put: v [
        self byteAt: i put: (v bitAnd: 255).
        self byteAt: i + 1 put: ((v bitShift: -8) bitAnd: 255).
        ^v
    ]
    intptrAt: i [
        | v b |
        v := 0.
        7 to: 0 by: -1 do: [ :k | b := self byteAt: i + k. v := (v bitShift: 8) bitOr: b ].
        ^v
    ]
    "The writer its reader has always needed. Every struct with a POINTER field
     is read-only without it — `MENUITEMINFOW>>dwTypeData:` is the first caller
     (DD10), and genstructs emits no setter for a pointer field because the
     runtime had none to emit."
    intptrAt: i put: v [
        | x |
        x := v.
        0 to: 7 do: [ :k |
            self byteAt: i + k put: (x bitAnd: 255).
            x := x bitShift: -8 ].
        ^v
    ]

    "A NON-OWNING window onto part of this block — a nested struct field
     (PAINTSTRUCT's rcPaint is a RECT at offset 12) is a view, not a copy, so
     writes through it reach the enclosing structure. `free` on a view is a
     no-op: the block belongs to whoever allocated it."
    viewAt: byteOffset size: n [
        ^ExternalMemory viewOn: self at: byteOffset size: n
    ]

    printOn: aStream [
        aStream nextPutAll: 'an ExternalMemory('.
        aStream nextPutAll: size printString.
        aStream nextPutAll: ' bytes @ '.
        aStream nextPutAll: address printString.
        aStream nextPutAll: ')'
    ]
]

"A UTF-16LE buffer holding a copy of a Smalltalk String, null-terminated, ready
 to pass as LPCWSTR.

 Dart strings ARE UTF-16 code units (DIALECT_GAPS A9), so this is a copy, not a
 transcoding — `asUnicodeValue` hands back the code unit directly. Characters
 outside the BMP are already surrogate PAIRS in the source string, so copying
 unit by unit preserves them with no special case."
ExternalMemory subclass: Utf16Buffer [
    Utf16Buffer class >> fromString: aString [
        | n buf cu |
        n := aString size.
        buf := self new: (n + 1) * 2.
        1 to: n do: [ :i |
            "`value` is this dialect's code-point accessor on Character
             (st/world/09_character.mst); Dolphin spells it asUnicodeValue,
             which is a compat alias DD8 will add rather than a name to assume."
            cu := (aString at: i) value.
            buf byteAt: (i - 1) * 2 + 1 put: (cu bitAnd: 255).
            buf byteAt: (i - 1) * 2 + 2 put: ((cu bitShift: -8) bitAnd: 255) ].
        buf byteAt: n * 2 + 1 put: 0.
        buf byteAt: n * 2 + 2 put: 0.
        ^buf
    ]

    "Read back a null-terminated UTF-16 string — the out-direction of the same
     marshalling (GetWindowTextW and friends fill a buffer we supplied).

     NOT named `asString`: that selector is a universal helper the IL builder
     rewrites at the call site, so a method of that name is never reached and
     the send silently answers the printString instead. Measured — the first
     version of this method was called `asString` and returned
     'an ExternalMemory(6 bytes @ …)'. Dolphin's own spelling is `asString`, so
     DD8's compat layer must route it here rather than define it."
    stringValue [
        | ws i cu done |
        ws := WriteStream on: String new.
        i := 0. done := false.
        [ done or: [ i * 2 + 2 > size ] ] whileFalse: [
            cu := (self byteAt: i * 2 + 1) bitOr: ((self byteAt: i * 2 + 2) bitShift: 8).
            cu = 0 ifTrue: [ done := true ]
                   ifFalse: [ ws nextPut: (Character value: cu). i := i + 1 ] ].
        ^ws contents
    ]
]
